A1. This kind of a form is always in good form. ~ BOYISH
A6. This is said for the buggy ride. ~ THANKS
A11. The life of the picnic. ~ ANT
A12. Comes out of a tree and goes into night clubs. ~ SAP
A14. This North American animal has very prominent teeth. ~ ELK
A15. A Roman foursome. ~ IV
A17. It's in the blood. ~ CORPUSCLE
A20. The inevitable conjunction. ~ OR
A21. An East Side "this." ~ DIS
A23. The guy who stole your wife. ~ PAL
A24. A good thing to keep beer on. ~ TAP
A25. What men have to do in order to be buried with military honors. ~ DIE
A26. To exhibit the wide open spaces. ~ GAPE
A28. Languished. ~ PINED
A30. Much shooting, fireworks and so forth, a festive occasion. ~ GALA
A31. Conjunction. ~ AND
A33. What second-hand car owners are continually in need of. ~ TOW
A34. That certain party. ~ HIM
A35. Real affection with a dash of spice. ~ ARDOR
A37. What these puzzles are (adv.). ~ FUNNY
A39. This got safely over the first water hazard. ~ ARK
A40. A good thing for a clean up. (Not an adv.) ~ LUX
A41. Radio made this famous. ~ AIR
A42. This made the world safe for something or other. ~ AEF
A43. To raise the spirits. (Nothing to do with beer hoists.) ~ ELATE
A46. Narrow openings. ~ SLITS
A49. Possessive pronoun. ~ ITS
A50. The center of things. ~ HUB
A52. This is tied to the feet in winter and acts much the same as a banana peel. ~ SKI
A54. An ice cream holder. ~ CONE
A56. Girl's name. ~ BELLA
A58. A long time (pl.). ~ EONS
A60. A month's old dress. ~ RAG
A61. An East Side girl. ~ GAL
A62. Kosh's first name. ~ OSH
A64. Negation. ~ NOT
A65. A baby's first name. ~ IT
A66. These are very speedy. ~ ANTELOPES
A69. Pronoun. ~ WE
A70. A space of time. ~ ERA
A71. A French name. ~ NOM
A72. This fellow is always the goat. ~ RAM
A74. Comely. ~ PRETTY
A75. When Dempsey fights. ~ SELDOM

D1. Steve Brody's famous take-off. ~ BRIDGE
D2. The delicatessen man's yes. ~ YA
D3. Incorporated (abbr.). ~ INC
D4. A slap is this kind of a signal. ~ STOP
D5. French water. ~ EAU
D7. Something people advertise for that they seldom get. ~ HELP
D8. Liquid refreshments. ~ ALE
D9. Two thirds of ink. ~ NK
D10. Something old-fashioned girls used to do. ~ SCREAM
D12. A kind of ticket and a kind of pea. ~ SPLIT
D13. A psychological stew. ~ PSTEW
D16. By way of. ~ VIA
D18. Something landlords do at the door. ~ RAP
D19. A blackguard. ~ CAD
D20. Try this on your flivver. ~ OIL
D22. This applies to eyes, fiction and Burgundy. ~ SPARKLING
D25. A contract for a hot future. ~ DAMNATION
D27. This comes last. ~ END
D29. Ancient Goddess of Night. (Don't worry about it.) ~ NOX
D30. The wages of this is breath. ~ GIN
D32. Several dumb-bells. ~ DOLTS
D34. Throws. ~ HURLS
D35. Existing for the present in plural numbers. ~ ARE
D36. This means that you'll be sorry. ~ RUE
D37. Three-quarters of a fish. ~ FIS
D38. Reply of a yes man. (Careful now!) ~ YES
D44. What the Knights of the Round Table did at noon. ~ ATE
D45. African underfed lizards. (init.) ~ AUL
D47. The fellow who looks like Mike. ~ IKE
D48. What most men have to do when they get married. ~ SCRIMP
D50. That Trojan dame, or maybe it was Sparta. ~ HELEN
D51. Something roses and scallions do in the spring. ~ BLOOM
D53. A good thing to hold your best girl in. ~ ESTEEM
D55. A wild grain. ~ OAT
D56. A wild time. ~ BAT
D57. Cleopatra's playful pet. ~ ASP
D59. This is the moment. ~ NOW
D61. A small two-winged fly. ~ GNAT
D63. In this place. ~ HERE
D66. This goes along with craft. ~ ART
D67. A snake's stomach. ~ LOW
D68. That alley girl. ~ SAL
D70. You can figure this out with ease. ~ EE
D73. A doctor's business letters. ~ MD


